{"kind":"assert","text":"E1 oracle feasibility audit — source only\n\nI read request d35ebede, promise 14b911fd and ruling 3316caa5 against source at 270dc8c7a69a4506fc3d5525339f08a4adb2803f. This audit changes no file and runs no producer, project import, compiler or test. It supplies source findings, not produced row counts, measured fixture size or validation evidence. Capture still waits for the actual NOTE END base and the owner's slot grant.\n\nCapture boundary and coverage:\n- The shared Ledger is packages/derive/test/fixtures.ts:87, exposed through @generalbusiness/dap-derive/testing; there is no packages/replay/test/fixtures.ts. Replay owners also reach it through world.ts, staging.ts, weighing.ts and archive-fixtures.ts. Keep every qualifying history owner and the derive judge owner; retain every reached call. Definition corpus declaration rows are not judgments.\n- packages/derive/src/judge.ts:184 and :189 export judgeAct and judgeActForReplay separately. Keep two lists and each real context. packages/replay/src/verify.ts:2440 calls the replay export through judgeText; withTextGaps at :2365 can call it repeatedly with different real text readers. Those are separate calls, not duplicates to discard. Record each owner's actual call count after capture; static declarations cannot supply it.\n\nCanonical inputs need a decision:\n- JudgeContext/Reading include StateView, own/history, text and snapshot readers, capability/platform rules, counts and observation callbacks (judge.ts:43; fields.ts:28,76; observes.ts:135; capability.ts:107; marks.ts:425). These close over state. Function names or Function.toString do not encode that state.\n- StateView.all() is not the full input. state.ts:330-336 explicitly excludes lookup indexes from that snapshot; private fixed rows/counts/hashes/readiness at :403-410 also affect reads. Calling all methods eagerly would add reads and could change lazy reader behavior.\n- CheckedSequence has a private brand and private scope/genesis state (packages/bytes/src/sequence.ts:10-29). A JSON clone does not carry its proof authority. Pass the original objects to the original judge. Do not replace proof readers or clocks with fixture substitutes.\n- The clock itself is data, {reading, behind, asOf} (derive/src/time.ts:21). Preserve those exact fields. ActJudgment can contain Settled rows with Map/Set fields (observes.ts:276); ordinary JSON.stringify can erase them. The existing bytes canonicalizer accepts JSON values, not arbitrary maps, sets, functions or branded instances.\n\nConcrete passive-capture candidate:\nUse a test-only setup module with the pinned Vitest vi.mock factory/importOriginal support (node_modules/vitest/dist/index.d.ts:429). Intercept the actual derive/src/judge.ts module before owner collection, preserve all other exports, and wrap only the two exports. Each wrapper calls its original exactly once. Snapshot the result before the owner can fold/seal or mutate referenced state. Intercepting only the package barrel could miss direct imports; the actual-module boundary needs a later owning witness. No production export or constructor registry is necessary on the source evidence reviewed.\n\nFor callback-backed inputs, the viable candidate is an explicitly tagged data graph plus a call-through observation transcript: preserve optional-field presence, Map/Set entries and order, proof public metadata and shared references; record each actual StateView/reader call's arguments and result while delegating to the original receiver once. Keep original branded proof objects in execution. Do not enumerate lazy iterators early or call readers a second time for logging. This transcript records the behavior the judge observes; it does not encode an arbitrary callback's unqueried closure state. Wrapping a reader can also change function/object identity. Thus this is a proposal, not a claim that the unchanged-callback condition already permits it. Planner must choose whether this observational representation satisfies “canonical input,” and whether transparent call-through wrappers satisfy “leave the proof and clock callbacks as they are.” If the condition requires unchanged reader identity and a complete serializable closure, the current APIs do not provide that representation. Do not silently hash only view.all(), opaque function labels or source text.\n\nFixture determinism and case identity:\nLedger fixes Actor secrets, T0, per-Ledger k0/k1 intent keys, explicit clock readings and fixture genesis nonces (fixtures.ts:87,136,184,211). Replay helpers use explicit nonce bytes; archive fake timers belong to their owner. A source search found no Date.now, Math.random, randomUUID, getRandomValues, Promise.all or concurrent() in replay/test plus the judge owner/core fixture. This supports deterministic inputs; it does not prove identical produced order or output. Keep owner/case-local indexes, not worker completion order. Do not add seeds, timing normalization or concurrency overrides.\nThe derive judge owner already makes judgments during describe collection: judge.test.ts:72-82 builds histories and calls did/share before leaf tests. getCurrentTest may then be undefined (pinned @vitest/runner/dist/index.d.ts:174-177). All those calls remain in scope. Planner must choose a stable explicit collection-case label, such as the owning suite plus a collection marker. Do not move setup into tests or drop collection calls to manufacture a leaf case.\n\nBoth-answer diagnostics:\nA committed hash cannot recover its baseline answer. The one permitted pre-split capture must also retain complete canonical input and baseline answer bytes, keyed by list/owner/case/index, outside the checkout. On mismatch the post-split comparator must verify the retained baseline bytes against the committed hashes and print the input, full baseline answer and full current answer. Running the old judge again would introduce a second oracle; printing only its digest would not print its answer.\nThis leaves a portability choice: a fresh checkout cannot print the baseline answer from hash rows alone. Planner must choose an explicit required external baseline input with a fail-closed missing-input result, or another approved committed encoding after the actual size report. No silent owner removal, normalization, sharding, compressed layout or second decoder/oracle is adopted here.\n\nSource size lower bound:\npackages/replay/test/floor.test.ts:15-29 reaches 150 runtime pulse judgments, one direct refusal judgment and 20 further pulse judgments each time fixture() completes. Three tests call that fixture (:40,:60,:68). Successful paths therefore reach at least 513 runtime judgeAct calls in that owner alone. Two 64-character hex SHA-256 strings per row already occupy 65,664 bytes, before owner/case/index/JSON overhead or any replay-list rows. This is a source lower bound for that straightforward hex-row encoding, not an actual produced fixture size or a lower bound for every possible encoding. Ruling 3316 already permits the one complete external capture followed by its actual byte-size report before any fixture commit. Preserve all rows; do not request another premature size approval or claim a measured result.\n\nOpen choices for root to refer: the faithful callback/view input representation and wrapper permission; collection-case labeling; and how fresh-checkout diagnostics obtain baseline raw answers. Capture, exact owner/count enumeration, actual size, the body split, E1 cases and the gate remain unrun.","body":{"scope":"E1 oracle feasibility; no producer or source edits","source_head":"270dc8c7a69a4506fc3d5525339f08a4adb2803f","status":"source-only","validation":"UNRUN"}}